Ukraine has launched new strikes against oil facilities deep inside Russian territory, according to President Volodymyr Zelenskyy, as Kyiv expands its campaign against infrastructure that supports Russia’s wartime economy. :contentReference[oaicite:1]{index=1}

The attacks reportedly targeted oil-related facilities in regions far from the battlefield, including areas such as Bashkortostan and the Yaroslavl region. Ukraine says these operations are designed to reduce Russia’s ability to finance and sustain its military campaign. :contentReference[oaicite:2]{index=2}

The strikes represent an escalation in Ukraine’s use of long-range weapons, demonstrating Kyiv’s ability to reach strategic targets hundreds of miles from the front line.

Military analysts say energy infrastructure has become a key target because Russia’s oil sector provides significant revenue that helps support government spending and military operations.

Zelenskyy has defended Ukraine’s long-range attacks as necessary measures to limit Russia’s ability to continue its invasion. Ukrainian officials say targeting refineries, fuel facilities and logistics networks can reduce resources available for Russian forces. :contentReference[oaicite:3]{index=3}

Kyiv has increasingly focused on energy infrastructure because fuel supplies are essential for military vehicles, aviation operations and logistics networks.

Ukraine has also targeted Russian maritime assets and vessels connected to oil transportation networks as part of broader efforts to pressure Moscow economically. :contentReference[oaicite:4]{index=4}

Russian officials have criticized the attacks, accusing Ukraine of striking economic infrastructure and creating risks for civilians.

A new report from Physicians for Human Rights Israel warns that the healthcare system in the occupied West Bank is facing a severe crisis, with hospitals, clinics and medical workers struggling under increasing financial and operational pressure. :contentReference[oaicite:1]{index=1}

The organization says a combination of funding shortages, restrictions on movement and disruptions affecting medical services has weakened the ability of healthcare providers to deliver consistent treatment.

The Palestinian Authority, which manages parts of the West Bank, has faced financial difficulties that have affected its ability to pay healthcare workers, purchase medicines and maintain public health facilities. According to the report, many public healthcare facilities have reduced operations because of these pressures. :contentReference[oaicite:2]{index=2}

Healthcare advocates warn that patients with chronic illnesses, emergency medical needs and limited financial resources are among those most affected.

Two Israeli soldiers were killed and four others injured in an explosion in southern Lebanon, marking the first Israeli military deaths since a June ceasefire with Hezbollah took effect. The incident occurred during an Israeli military operation in the area, with the cause of the explosion under investigation. :contentReference[oaicite:1]{index=1}

The deaths have increased concerns that violence along the Israel-Lebanon border could escalate despite ongoing diplomatic efforts aimed at maintaining stability.

The Israeli military said the incident occurred while forces were operating in southern Lebanon, an area where tensions have remained high despite the truce. Officials are investigating whether the explosion was caused by an explosive device or another form of attack. :contentReference[oaicite:2]{index=2}

The casualties represent a significant setback for efforts to maintain calm after months of fighting between Israel and Hezbollah.

The June truce was intended to reduce hostilities between Israel and Hezbollah and create conditions for broader security arrangements along the border. However, both sides have continued accusing each other of violations. :contentReference[oaicite:3]{index=3}

Following the deaths of the Israeli soldiers, Israel launched strikes in southern Lebanon. Lebanese authorities reported casualties from the strikes, adding to concerns about renewed escalation. :contentReference[oaicite:4]{index=4}

The latest violence comes as diplomatic discussions continue over implementing parts of the ceasefire agreement, including security arrangements and the future deployment of forces in southern Lebanon. :contentReference[oaicite:5]{index=5}

Analysts say incidents like this demonstrate how fragile the ceasefire remains despite political efforts to prevent a return to full-scale conflict.

The renewed violence occurred as officials were engaged in discussions aimed at strengthening the ceasefire framework. Negotiators have been working on arrangements involving Israeli troop positions, Lebanese security forces and broader border stability. :contentReference[oaicite:6]{index=6}

The talks highlight the difficulty of maintaining agreements in regions where armed groups, governments and international actors have competing interests.

Hezbollah has not been directly involved in all diplomatic discussions, creating additional challenges for enforcement and communication. :contentReference[oaicite:7]{index=7}

Regional officials fear that continued exchanges of attacks could undermine progress and trigger a wider confrontation.
